Loom-Core

Creative Catalyst: The scene bloomed in a ghost mall where teenagers rewired abandoned arcade cabinets.

Loom-Core sounds like a high-octane heartbreak. It marries the aggressive, fuzzed-out guitar riffing of 1990s hard rock with the hyper-melodic, major-seventh-heavy chord progressions of J-pop. The sonic glue is the Yamaha CS-80; its legendary polyphonic aftertouch is used here to create thick, weeping synth pads that "choke" the guitar distortion. It is simultaneously crushing and fragile, evoking the feeling of watching a sunset from the roof of a condemned department store.

Genre DNA
*   Tempo & Pulse: 145–160 BPM, featuring driving, rigid 4/4 drums that lean into a "wall of sound" aesthetic.
*   Harmonic Palette: Heavy reliance on Major 7th, Add9, and Sus4 chords; often utilizes deceptive cadences that shift between minor key verses and soaring, bright major-key choruses.
*   Production Signatures: "Tape-warped" guitar tracks that sound like they were recorded onto degrading VHS; side-chaining the CS-80 pads to the snare drum; high-frequency vocal layering that mimics the "sparkle" of early 90s digital pop production.
*   Dynamics: Extreme contrasts; verses are sparse, featuring only bass and lonely synth leads, exploding into stadium-sized, multi-tracked guitar and synth-drenched choruses.

Scene & Origin Lore
The movement began in the winter of 1996 within the hollowed-out atrium of the Oshima Heights Mall. Forbidden from entry, local teenagers bypassed the security gates and set up shop in the food court, cannibalizing the circuit boards of Street Fighter II and Ridge Racer cabinets to stabilize their analog synths. The fashion was a collage of school uniforms and thrifted, oversized workwear, reflecting the "unfinished" nature of their environment. Rituals involved "The Patching," where the collective would spend entire nights soldering custom oscillators to the mall’s PA system, creating a sound that was simultaneously a celebration of industrial decay and a desperate reach for the pop-stardom they were excluded from.

Instruments: Yamaha CS-80 (the primary emotive voice), heavily distorted humbucker-equipped electric guitars, programmed Roland TR-909 drum machines, and fretless electric bass.

